/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  solidDryer02_starved_air / flowsheetDict   (LEAF node)

  solidDryer01_sugar with its hot air CUT FROM 800 TO 100 kmol/h -- the same
  wet sugar (10 kmol/h sucrose, 38 kmol/h bound water, X_in = 0.20, at
  330 K), the same air (420 K, 2 mol% water), one eighth of it.

  WHAT IT SHOWS: the third limit of the solid dryer, SATURATION.  The air
  can still PAY for the isotherm's 680.8 kg/h -- its sensible heat plus the
  warm sugar's would cool the exhaust to ~318.6 K -- but 100 kmol/h of air
  at 318.6 K cannot CARRY that water: it saturates (a_w = 1) at about
  157 kg/h.  The dryer stops there, the powder leaves at X ~0.15 kg/kg
  instead of the isotherm's X_eq ~0.001, and the run says so (the
  `[saturation-bound]` result block and a WARNING in the caveat block).
  Until 2026-10-05 (DEV.md C37 item 3) the unit evaporated all 680.8 kg/h,
  priced the exhaust at its condensed equilibrium -- a fog carrying ~520 kg/h
  of liquid water -- and published it as a vapour, vf = 1.

  The outlet temperature barely moves between the two answers: a fog and a
  saturated gas beside the same water left on the powder hold the same
  enthalpy.  What moves is WHERE the water is -- which is the whole point.
  Witness of check_evaporative_dryer arm (g).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
name        solidDryer;
type        solidDryer;

boundary    { inlets ( wetSolid  hotAir );  outlets ( drySolid  humidExhaust ); }

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only
